d Republicans, or consolidation of anti-incumbent and independent voters behind Tang Williams, could narrow what polls currently show as a wide margin.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Maggie Goodlander (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Democratic incumbent Maggie Goodlander enters the general election with clear advantages in New Hampshire's second district. Recent polls from the University of New Hampshire and Saint Anselm College show her leading Lily Tang Williams by fifteen to twenty-one points among likely voters, consistent with surveys going back a year. Every major rating outfit places the seat at Likely or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price her win odds above ninety percent.\n\nGoodlander has raised roughly $4.2 million to Tang Williams' $1.2 million and holds a large cash-on-hand edge. The district leans only modestly Democratic, and Tang Williams is a known quantity in a rematch, but the polling, ratings, and money all point the same direction. A minor independent candidate is on the ballot but is not viewed as a significant factor.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9818, "margin": 13.85}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9772, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.978, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9829, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9791, "margin": 15.0}]}